Implement KJ_TRY / KJ_CATCH macros

This implements separate macros of the forms:

```cpp
KJ_TRY {
  // throws
} KJ_CATCH(e) {
  // handle
}
```

The big killer features over our existing utilities are that:

- It coerces all exceptions to kj::Exception for you.
- You can co_await in both the "try" and the "catch" blocks, if you're in a coroutine.
- You can use other standard control flow keywords in either block, such as `return`ing from the enclosing function, `break`ing an outer loop, etc.

The macros rely on a trick: you can `goto` from an `if` statement's true branch to its false branch. This allows us (the implementor) to execute a compound statement inline first, like a `try/catch` block, and only conditionally jump to the user's provided subsequent block. I learned the trick from this article by Simon Tatham: https://www.chiark.greenend.org.uk/~sgtatham/mp/

The separate macro approach requires hard-coding a constant identifier to serve as the exception storage (`_kjTryCatchExc

